\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Learning Kubernetes from Docker Compose and Docker Swarm is a great way to build upon your existing knowledge and get a smoother transition into Kubernetes. Both Docker Compose and Docker Swarm are useful tools for container orchestration, but Kubernetes is more complex and offers a broader set of features. Here\rquote s a learning path to help you use your current understanding of Docker and Docker Swarm to get started with Kubernetes.\par
\par
Step 1: Understand Kubernetes Basics\par
Before diving into running Kubernetes itself, it\rquote s important to understand the basic concepts that Kubernetes uses. These concepts are similar to what you might have encountered in Docker Compose and Docker Swarm, but Kubernetes has a more complex structure and features.\par
\par
Key Kubernetes Concepts:\par
Pod: The smallest and simplest Kubernetes object. A pod represents a single instance of a running process in your cluster and can contain one or more containers.\par
Node: A physical or virtual machine in your Kubernetes cluster. Each node contains the necessary components to run pods (i.e., Kubelet, container runtime like Docker or containerd, etc.).\par
Cluster: A set of nodes that run containerized applications in Kubernetes.\par
Deployment: A way to define a set of identical pods. A deployment ensures that the specified number of pods are always running.\par
Service: A way to expose an application running on a set of pods as a network service. It defines how to access pods from within or outside the cluster.\par
Namespace: A way to divide cluster resources between multiple users.\par
Ingress: A set of rules for managing external access to services, usually HTTP.\par
ConfigMap and Secret: Ways to store and manage configuration data and sensitive data, respectively, that your applications need.\par
Resources to Learn the Basics:\par
Kubernetes Concepts\par
Kubernetes Overview\par
\par
Step 2: Set Up Kubernetes Locally\par
You need to install a local Kubernetes environment to begin experimenting. Here are some options for setting up Kubernetes locally:\par
\par
\cf1 Minikube:\par
\cf0\par
Minikube is an easy-to-setup tool that runs a Kubernetes cluster locally on your machine. It's great for learning and development.\par
\par
Install Minikube:\par
# For Linux or macOS:\par
curl -LO {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64 }}{\fldrslt{https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs22\par
sudo mv minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube\par
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/minikube\par
\par
Start a Kubernetes cluster with:\par
minikube start\par
\par
\cf1 Docker Desktop Kubernetes:\par
\cf0\par
If you are already using Docker Desktop on Windows or macOS, Kubernetes is built into Docker Desktop. You can enable it in the Docker Desktop settings under Kubernetes.\par
Once enabled, you can use the kubectl command (installed with Docker Desktop) to interact with Kubernetes.\par
\par
\cf1 Kind (Kubernetes in Docker):\par
\cf0\par
Kind is a tool for running Kubernetes clusters inside Docker containers, which is great for testing and learning purposes.\par
\par
Install Kind:\par
curl -Lo ./kind {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kind/releases/download/v0.18.0/kind-linux-amd64 }}{\fldrslt{https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kind/releases/download/v0.18.0/kind-linux-amd64\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs22\par
chmod +x ./kind\par
sudo mv ./kind /usr/local/bin/kind\par
\par
Create a Kubernetes cluster:\par
kind create cluster\par
\par
Step 3: Transition from Docker Compose to Kubernetes\par
Since you\rquote re already familiar with Docker Compose, transitioning from Docker Compose to Kubernetes manifests will be easier.\par
\par
Docker Compose to Kubernetes:\par
Docker Compose is great for running multi-container applications on a single host, but Kubernetes is designed for scaling and managing containers across a cluster of machines.\par
Kubernetes manifests (YAML files) define the desired state for your applications. You can think of them as the Kubernetes equivalent of a docker-compose.yml file, but with more complexity and options.\par
Example: Docker Compose to Kubernetes Transition\par
Let\rquote s say your docker-compose.yml for your 3 containers looks like this:\par
\par
yaml\par
\par
version: '3.8'\par
\par
services:\par
  ems_ui:\par
    image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:u1\par
    ports:\par
      - "7000:7000"\par
    networks:\par
      - ems_network\par
\par
  ems_identity:\par
    image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:i1\par
    ports:\par
      - "9000:9000"\par
    networks:\par
      - ems_network\par
\par
  ems_employee:\par
    image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:e1\par
    ports:\par
      - "8000:8000"\par
    networks:\par
      - ems_network\par
\par
networks:\par
  ems_network:\par
    driver: bridge\par
You can convert this to Kubernetes YAML manifests like this:\par
\par
\cf1 Kubernetes Deployment Example:\cf0\par
yaml\par
\par
apiVersion: apps/v1\par
kind: Deployment\par
metadata:\par
  name: ems-identity\par
spec:\par
  replicas: 1\par
  selector:\par
    matchLabels:\par
      app: ems-identity\par
  template:\par
    metadata:\par
      labels:\par
        app: ems-identity\par
    spec:\par
      containers:\par
        - name: ems-identity\par
          image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:i1\par
          ports:\par
            - containerPort: 9000\par
---\par
apiVersion: apps/v1\par
kind: Deployment\par
metadata:\par
  name: ems-ui\par
spec:\par
  replicas: 1\par
  selector:\par
    matchLabels:\par
      app: ems-ui\par
  template:\par
    metadata:\par
      labels:\par
        app: ems-ui\par
    spec:\par
      containers:\par
        - name: ems-ui\par
          image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:u1\par
          ports:\par
            - containerPort: 7000\par
---\par
apiVersion: apps/v1\par
kind: Deployment\par
metadata:\par
  name: ems-employee\par
spec:\par
  replicas: 1\par
  selector:\par
    matchLabels:\par
      app: ems-employee\par
  template:\par
    metadata:\par
      labels:\par
        app: ems-employee\par
    spec:\par
      containers:\par
        - name: ems-employee\par
          image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:e1\par
          ports:\par
            - containerPort: 8000\par
\par
\cf1 Kubernetes Service Example:\cf0\par
Kubernetes services expose your deployments to the network:\par
\par
yaml\par
\par
apiVersion: v1\par
kind: Service\par
metadata:\par
  name: ems-ui-service\par
spec:\par
  selector:\par
    app: ems-ui\par
  ports:\par
    - protocol: TCP\par
      port: 7000\par
      targetPort: 7000\par
---\par
apiVersion: v1\par
kind: Service\par
metadata:\par
  name: ems-identity-service\par
spec:\par
  selector:\par
    app: ems-identity\par
  ports:\par
    - protocol: TCP\par
      port: 9000\par
      targetPort: 9000\par
---\par
apiVersion: v1\par
kind: Service\par
metadata:\par
  name: ems-employee-service\par
spec:\par
  selector:\par
    app: ems-employee\par
  ports:\par
    - protocol: TCP\par
      port: 8000\par
      targetPort: 8000\par
\par
\cf1 Apply the YAML Manifests:\cf0\par
After creating the Kubernetes YAML files for your deployments and services, you can apply them using kubectl:\par
\par
bash\par
\par
kubectl apply -f ems-ui-deployment.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f ems-identity-deployment.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f ems-employee-deployment.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f ems-ui-service.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f ems-identity-service.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f ems-employee-service.yaml\par
\par
Step 4: Explore Kubernetes Features\par
Once you get your containers running, explore more Kubernetes features:\par
\par
Scaling: You can scale your deployments up or down easily:\par
kubectl scale deployment ems-ui --replicas=3\par
\par
Namespaces: Group your resources by namespace for better organization.\par
kubectl create namespace ems\par
kubectl apply -f ems-ui-deployment.yaml --namespace=ems\par
\par
Helm: Helm is a package manager for Kubernetes, similar to docker-compose but with more features and flexibility.\par
\par
Learn Helm.\par
Ingress: Manage external HTTP access to your services using an ingress controller.\par
\par
Learn more about Ingress.\par
Step 5: Practice and Learn More\par
The best way to learn Kubernetes is through hands-on practice. Here are some resources to continue your learning journey:\par
\par
Kubernetes Documentation: The official Kubernetes documentation is your best friend.\par
\par
Kubernetes Docs\par
Kubernetes on Google Cloud: Google Cloud offers a free tier for Kubernetes clusters. You can experiment with Kubernetes in a cloud environment.\par
\par
Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)\par
Kubernetes by Example: Free resource for hands-on Kubernetes tutorials.\par
\par
Kubernetes By Example\par
Interactive Learning:\par
\par
[Play with Kubernetes](https\par
